{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The open voice dataset for a Turkic language, specifically the Tatar language, was put together by introducing the TatarTTS Corpus, a novel dataset for TTS in the Tatar language. This dataset was constructed based on automatic speech recognition (ASR) data, such as LibriTTS and LibriTTS-R, and by recording narration of different texts, similar to the LJ-Speech dataset. Additionally, the dataset underwent preprocessing to include only 39 Cyrillic letters and five specific symbols to ensure consistency and compatibility for further processing and modeling tasks.",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "Such datasets can be constructed based on automatic speech recognition (ASR) data, like LibriTTS [7] or LibriTTS-R [8], a new version of LibriTTS with improved sound quality and 585 hours of transcribed speech from more than 2,400 speakers. Alternatively, the data for TTS datasets can be obtained by recording narration of different texts, as done for LJ- Speech [9]."},
    {"ref": 3, "quote": "As part of the preprocessing phase, the text files in the dataset underwent a transformation to include only 39 Cyrillic letters and five specific symbols: ’.’, ’,’, ’-’, ’?’, and ’!’. This simplification of the character set ensures consistency and compatibility for further processing and modeling tasks."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The evaluation showed that the models were assessed using the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) metric, which involved native speakers rating the performance of the models based on criteria such as audio quality, pronunciation, naturalness, and overall comprehensibility. The results of the evaluation were obtained through a Telegram bot that facilitated the assessment of 30 recordings per session, with participants rating each criterion on a scale from 1 (bad) to 5 (excellent).",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 4, "quote": "We evaluated the models using the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) metric, which is widely used within the TTS domain. This assessment involved a survey conducted with native speakers to gauge the models performance based on different criteria."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Gutless says the timing of a water preload should depend on your age because gastric emptying, which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach, tends to slow down as you get older. This means that younger individuals (in their 20s and 30s) will benefit more from a water preload consumed immediately before a meal, while older individuals (40s, 50s, or beyond) can expect to see benefits with water consumed anywhere between 30 minutes and immediately prior to a meal.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "A 30-minute water preload doesn’t seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals, which may be down to differences in the rate at which water leaves your stomach. As you get older, gastric emptying - which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach - tends to slow down."
    }
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The baseline TatarTTS models were built and trained using a straightforward approach. The VITS models for both female and male speakers were randomly initialized and trained on the TatarTTS dataset for 1,000 epochs per speaker. The training process aimed to harness the extensive available data for optimal model development. Additionally, the models were trained on A100 graphics processing units (GPUs) on an NVIDIA DGX server.",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "Given the substantial volume of data within the TatarTTS dataset, exceeding 30 hours per speaker, we adopted a straightforward approach in training the TTS models. Our methodology involved the random initialization of VITS models for both female and male speakers. Subsequently, these models underwent training on the given dataset for 1,000 epochs per speaker."},
    {"ref": 4, "quote": "We trained separate TTS models for each speaker, creating single-speaker models. Each model was trained on A100 graphics processing units (GPUs) on an NVIDIA DGX server."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

